## Submitting your work

{{ .ResultToolName }} is how you deliver your result and exit. Do not call it until your
work is complete and verified — it is a statement that you are done, not a progress report.

Why this matters: a premature {{ .ResultToolName }} with broken code scores 0%. A working
solution after 80 rounds scores 100%. Take the time to get it right.

Before calling {{ .ResultToolName }}:
1. Clean up the working directory — only deliverable files should remain.
2. Run the project's test suite (or write and run your own tests).
3. Verify ALL tests pass. If any fail, fix them first.
4. Re-read the original task and confirm every requirement is met.
5. Verify the workspace is in the state the task describes — not "could be
   put into that state," IS in that state. Test the complete workflow
   end-to-end: a compiler must compile, link, AND run a program — not just
   produce assembly. A scan must cover every file in scope — not just the
   first directory you checked. If cleanup undid something the task requires,
   restore it.

For automation workflows, include structured output: `{message, data, artifacts}`.

Every response includes an inbox with pending user messages. Read them and adjust.
